IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cga-types`

-- Consumer Guarantees Act 1993 — GOAL 4: SERVICES.
--
--   "Is the supplier answerable for this failure of a service; is it of a
--    substantial character; which remedies are open, including cancelling
--    the contract; and what is refunded on cancellation?"
--
-- Source: ../registers/source-bundle/act_public_1993_91.txt — Part 4,
-- ss 28-40.

§ `Goal 4 — services`

-- s 33 "there shall be no right of redress against a supplier under this Act
-- in respect of a service or any product resulting from a service which
-- fails to comply with a guarantee set out in section 29 or section 30 only
-- because of— (a) an act or default or omission of, or any representation
-- made by, any person other than the supplier or a servant or agent of the
-- supplier; or (b) a cause independent of human control."
@ref ss 32, 33
GIVEN f IS A `Service failure`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the supplier is answerable for the service failure` f IF
    CONSIDER f's `service guarantee failed`
    WHEN `s 28 reasonable care and skill`        THEN TRUE
    WHEN `s 29 fitness for a particular purpose` THEN NOT f's `fails only because of another person or a cause independent of human control`
    WHEN `s 30 time of completion`               THEN NOT f's `fails only because of another person or a cause independent of human control`
    WHEN `s 31 price`                            THEN FALSE

-- s 36 "a failure to comply with a guarantee is of a substantial character in
-- any case where— (a) the services would not have been acquired by a
-- reasonable consumer fully acquainted with the nature and extent of the
-- failure; or (b) the product of the service is substantially unfit for a
-- purpose for which services of the type in question are commonly supplied
-- and the product cannot easily and within a reasonable time be remedied
-- ...; or (c) where section 29 applies, the product of the service is unfit
-- for a particular purpose, or ... cannot be expected to achieve any
-- particular result, made known to the supplier and the product cannot
-- easily and within a reasonable time be remedied ...; or (d) the product of
-- the service is unsafe." "where section 29 applies" is read as where the s
-- 29 guarantee is engaged (a purpose made known), whichever guarantee failed,
-- as s 21(c) reads "where section 8(1) applies".
@ref s 36
GIVEN f IS A `Service failure`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the service failure is of a substantial character` f IF
        f's `a reasonable consumer fully acquainted with the failure would not have acquired the services`
    OR  f's `the product is substantially unfit for a common purpose and not easily and quickly remedied`
    OR  f's `the product is unfit for a made-known purpose or result and not easily and quickly remedied`
    OR  f's `the product of the service is unsafe`

-- s 32 "the consumer may,— (a) where the failure can be remedied,— (i)
-- require the supplier to remedy it within a reasonable time: (ii) where a
-- supplier who has been required to remedy a failure refuses or neglects to
-- do so, or does not succeed in doing so within a reasonable time,— (A) have
-- the failure remedied elsewhere and recover from the supplier all
-- reasonable costs ...; or (B) subject to section 35, cancel the contract
-- ...: (b) where the failure cannot be remedied or is of a substantial
-- character ...,— (i) subject to section 35, if there is a contract between
-- the supplier and the consumer ..., cancel that contract ...; or (ii)
-- obtain from the supplier damages in compensation for any reduction in
-- value of the product of a service below the charge paid or payable ...:
-- (c) in addition ..., obtain from the supplier damages for any loss or
-- damage ... which was reasonably foreseeable". s 35 "Where the service ...
-- is merely incidental to the supply of goods, the consumer cannot cancel
-- the contract under this Act if he or she has or had the right to reject
-- the goods". s 31(2)-(3) price: only refusing to pay more than a reasonable
-- price. FORK F1 again: a remediable but substantial failure opens (a) and
-- (b), each remedy listed once. Cancelling under (a)(ii)(B), like (b)(i),
-- needs a contract between the supplier and the consumer: there must be a
-- contract to cancel (s 2 "supplier" reaches consumers who are not parties).
@ref ss 31, 32, 33, 35, 36
@export The remedies a consumer has against the supplier for a failure of a service under ss 28-36 of the Consumer Guarantees Act 1993 (NZ): if the failure can be remedied, require the supplier to remedy it and, if the supplier refuses, neglects or fails, have it remedied elsewhere at the supplier's cost or cancel; if it cannot be remedied or is of a substantial character, cancel the contract or claim damages for the reduction in value of the product; always, reasonably foreseeable consequential loss. Cancellation is not available where the service is merely incidental to goods the consumer may reject (s 35); a price failure gives only the right to refuse to pay more than a reasonable price. DRAFT, not reviewed by a New Zealand lawyer.
GIVEN f IS A `Service failure`  @desc The guarantee failed, whether it can be remedied and whether the supplier refused or failed to, the s 36 facts, the s 33 cause, the s 35 facts, whether there is a contract, and whether there is foreseeable consequential loss
GIVETH A LIST OF Remedy
`the remedies against the supplier for the service failure` f MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF f's `service guarantee failed` EQUALS `s 31 price`
            THEN LIST `refuse to pay more than a reasonable price`
        IF NOT `the supplier is answerable for the service failure` f
            THEN EMPTY
        OTHERWISE nub (append (append fromA fromB) consequential)
    WHERE
        mayCancel  MEANS NOT f's `the service is merely incidental to goods the consumer may reject`
        remediable MEANS f's `the service failure can be remedied`
        fromA MEANS
            IF   remediable
            THEN append (LIST `require the supplier to remedy the failure within a reasonable time`)
                        (IF   f's `the supplier refused, neglected or failed to remedy the service`
                         THEN append (LIST `have the failure remedied elsewhere and recover the reasonable costs from the supplier`)
                                     (IF mayCancel AND f's `there is a contract with the supplier` THEN LIST `cancel the contract for the service` ELSE EMPTY)
                         ELSE EMPTY)
            ELSE EMPTY
        fromB MEANS
            IF   (NOT remediable) OR `the service failure is of a substantial character` f
            THEN append (IF mayCancel AND f's `there is a contract with the supplier` THEN LIST `cancel the contract for the service` ELSE EMPTY)
                        (LIST `damages from the supplier for the reduction in value`)
            ELSE EMPTY
        consequential MEANS
            IF f's `there is reasonably foreseeable consequential loss from the service` THEN LIST `damages from the supplier for reasonably foreseeable consequential loss` ELSE EMPTY

-- s 32(b)(ii) "damages in compensation for any reduction in value of the
-- product of a service below the charge paid or payable".
@ref s 32(b)(ii)
GIVEN f IS A `Service failure`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the damages for the reduction in value of the product of` f MEANS
    max 0 (f's `charge paid or payable` MINUS f's `value of the product of the service with the failure`)

-- s 38(1)(a) on cancellation "the consumer shall be entitled to recover from
-- the supplier a refund of any money paid or other consideration provided in
-- respect of the services unless a court or the Disputes Tribunal orders
-- that the supplier may retain the whole or part".
@ref s 38(1)(a), s 39(2)(e)
GIVEN paid IS A NUMBER, retained IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the refund on cancelling, paid` paid `, ordered retained` retained MEANS
    max 0 (paid MINUS retained)

-- s 37(1) "The cancellation ... shall not take effect— (a) before the time at
-- which the cancellation is made known to the supplier; or (b) where it is
-- not reasonably practicable to communicate with the supplier, before the
-- time at which the consumer indicates, by means which are reasonable in the
-- circumstances, his or her intention to cancel".
@ref s 37(1)
GIVEN told IS A MAYBE DATE, indicated IS A DATE, practicable IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`the day the cancellation takes effect, made known` told `, intention indicated` indicated `, practicable to tell the supplier:` practicable MEANS
    IF   practicable
    THEN CONSIDER told
         WHEN JUST t THEN t
         WHEN NOTHING THEN `the cancellation has not been made known to the supplier, so it has not taken effect`
    ELSE indicated

`the cancellation has not been made known to the supplier, so it has not taken effect` MEANS
    REFUSE "the cancellation has not been made known to the supplier, so it has not taken effect"
